Easily my favorite part about this job is traveling to unfamiliar places and talking to strangers. Even if the interview doesn't go well, and some of them haven't, trust me. As it turns out, cops don't love it when you ask them a thousand questions about why they couldn't solve a murder. But one thing I've learned over the years is if the person you're talking to walks out of their house to greet you rather than waiting for you to come to the door, it means the interview is going to go just fine. And that's how we were greeted by Michael Wynn. My name is Michael Wynn, and I guess these days I'm the unofficial historian for Central Louisiana. After a long career in law enforcement, Michael spends most of his free time researching local history.
我这份工作最喜欢的部分无疑是去到不熟悉的地方并和陌生人交谈。即使采访进展不顺——相信我，有些确实不顺。事实证明，当你问警察成千上万个关于他们为什么破不了谋杀案的问题时，他们并不太高兴。但我多年来学到的一件事是，如果你交谈的人走出家门来迎接你，而不是等你在门口敲门，这意味着采访会非常顺利。这就是迈克尔·温迎接我们的方式。我叫迈克尔·温，我想如今我是中路易斯安那州的非官方历史学家。在经历了漫长的执法生涯后，迈克尔把大部分空闲时间都花在了研究地方历史上。

